module gunbc.roadmap_identity

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr, Bool, Int, List }

data roadmap_identity_law: String = "A roadmap node's identity is MINTED, never derived. Slug, title, criteria digest, containment position, dependency neighbourhood, declaration order, and file position are FORBIDDEN identity inputs — the same law std.occurrence_identity states for occurrences, generalized to an entity that must outlive the graph it is read from.\n\nThe law is reused; the TYPE deliberately is not. std.occurrence_identity.OccurrenceId is an ordinal from a per-graph allocator that starts at zero (OccurrenceIdAllocator { next_id: 0 }, alloc returns next_id then increments), so it is unique only INSIDE one constructed graph. Reconstructing the roadmap with a node inserted at the front renumbers every node after it, and two concurrent branches both mint the same next integer. That is positional identity wearing an allocator's clothes: correct for occurrences within one parse, wrong for a work item that must survive edits, reordering, regeneration, and independent branches. What occurrence identity deliberately does not claim is PERSISTENCE SCOPE, and that is exactly what a roadmap node needs.\n\nThe minted value is therefore collision-resistant rather than sequential, and it is persisted in the authority at authoring time. Nothing mints during rendering, compilation, or regeneration — a value that could be re-derived by evaluation would reintroduce the property this module exists to remove."

data roadmap_identity_five_axes_note: String = "Five things the roadmap must keep separate, because collapsing any pair is what makes replanning destructive: IDENTITY (which work item this is — never changes); SLUG (what it is currently called — may change); CRITERIA DIGEST (which acceptance bar applies — changes, and correctly invalidates a stale receipt); CONTAINMENT (what decomposes it); DEPENDENCY (what must precede it). Today identity and slug are the same value, so a rename is a delete plus a create: edges stop resolving, acceptance receipts name a node that no longer exists, and any dispatch attempt or session joined to it is orphaned. That is not hypothetical — the 2026-07-27 authority refresh deleted the node carrying the only execution contract and no claim noticed. Separating the two makes a rename a label edit and nothing more.\n\nCriteria digest stays a content hash and that is correct: it is not identifying the node, it is fingerprinting the bar so an old acceptance cannot silently survive a changed one. Content-addressing is the right tool for revision and the forbidden tool for identity; the roadmap needs both, keyed differently."

type RoadmapNodeIdentity {
  value: NonEmptyStr
}

type RoadmapNodeSlug {
  value: NonEmptyStr
}

type RoadmapIdentityBinding {
  identity: RoadmapNodeIdentity
  slug: RoadmapNodeSlug
}

data roadmap_identity_additive_stage_note: String = "STAGE I0, deliberately additive. This module introduces the identity space and binds one persisted value to every declared node; it changes no rendering, no dependency edge, no acceptance receipt and no dispatch path. The v1-deletion recut can therefore be authored against durable identities immediately, without the recut waiting on a full consumer migration — and without every new node and edge being rewritten a second time once the migration lands. Consumers move in later stages: edges and receipts, then explicit containment, then runtime/dispatch, then the slug-as-identity constructors delete."

